%% CASE Halden Chemicals safety upgrade
Halden Chemicals is upgrading the control and safety systems of a chlorine production unit at a site regulated as a major-hazard installation. Hardware and installation follow a predictive plan; an agile team builds a new digital operations dashboard.

- Corporate rules require every project at a major-hazard site to pass an **independent stage-gate review** before construction starts.
- Any change to safety systems must go through the site's **management-of-change (MOC)** process, and the regulator must be notified before restart.
- A hazard and operability study (HAZOP) of the new design produced **40 recommendations**, **6** of them rated high-risk.
- Control room operators will move from panel controls to screens. Experienced operators worry that they will lose their overview of the process.
@@ BE1 | Predictive
The contractor is ready to start construction, but the independent stage-gate review has not yet taken place. What should the project manager do?
A) Wait for the review, as corporate governance requires, and prepare for it promptly
B) Start construction now and hold the review in parallel, so that no time is lost
C) Ask the contractor to start with preparatory work that the review would not cover
D) Ask the site manager to waive the review, since the contractor is already mobilised
= A
? Governance gates exist to confirm readiness before major commitments. Starting before the review, or working around it, undermines the control that it provides.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
During installation, engineers want to change a safety interlock setting to speed up commissioning. What must happen?
A) It goes through management of change for review and approval before it is made
B) The engineers make the change and record it in the commissioning log for later review by the team
C) The project manager approves the change verbally, since commissioning is running behind schedule
D) The change is made temporarily and reversed after commissioning, without the need for any review
= A
? In major-hazard operations, changes to safety systems, even temporary ones, are reviewed and approved through management of change before they are made.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
How should the six high-risk HAZOP recommendations be handled?
A) Assign owners, design responses and track each one to verified closure before restart
B) Address them after restart, once the plant has returned to normal production
C) Treat all 40 recommendations the same and close them in the order in which they were raised
D) Accept them, since the HAZOP team included experienced engineers from the plant
= A
? High-risk recommendations need named owners, effective responses and verified closure before hazardous operation resumes.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
How should the project address the operators' worry about losing their overview of the process?
A) Involve operators in designing and testing the screens, and train them on a simulator before go-live
B) Keep the old control panels in the control room as a backup, so that operators can use them if needed
C) Tell the operators that modern screens are better than panels, so their worries are unfounded
D) Train the operators on the new screens during the first week after the system goes live
= A
? Involving the people affected in the design and giving them safe practice before go-live addresses real concerns and builds confidence.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
A security risk cannot be fully mitigated before a release. Who should formally accept the residual risk?
A) The risk owner with the authority to accept it, as governance defines
B) The developer who wrote the code in which the vulnerability was found
C) The scrum master, since the scrum master is responsible for the process
D) The tester who found the vulnerability during the security testing
= A
? Accepting residual risk is a governance decision made by someone with the authority and accountability to do so.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
A new research computing service will serve all faculties. Who should decide its priorities after launch?
A) A service owner, supported by a board representing the faculties
B) The faculty that shouts loudest about its needs each month
C) The project team, which will stay in place after the launch
D) The vendor that supplies the service's hardware and software
= A
? Shared services need clear ownership and representative governance, so that priorities are set fairly and transparently.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Who should accept the new crew scheduling system into operations?
A) The operational owner, against agreed operational acceptance criteria
B) The project manager, once the project's testing has been completed
C) The vendor, since it built and configured the scheduling system
D) The internal audit department, after an audit of the project
= A
? Operational acceptance is given by the business owner who will run the system, based on agreed criteria such as support readiness and performance.

@@ BE1 | Agile
Several product owners compete for the same platform team's capacity. Which governance mechanism helps most?
A) A forum that agrees priorities against shared objectives, with a final say
B) A rule that the platform team works on whichever request arrived first, whatever its value
C) Letting the platform team choose the requests that it finds most interesting to work on
D) Splitting the platform team equally between the product owners, whatever their needs
= A
? Shared capacity is allocated through transparent governance that compares requests against common objectives, with clear authority for the final decision.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Which combined log is often reviewed by steering committees to track risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies?
A) A RAID log
B) A burndown chart
C) A RACI chart
D) A product backlog
= A
? A RAID log brings risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies together, which gives governance bodies a compact view of what needs attention.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid | multi
A personal data breach has been confirmed. Which two actions does data protection law typically require? (Choose two.)
A) Notifying the data protection authority within the legal deadline
B) Informing the affected people when the risk to them is high
C) Deleting the logs that show how the breach happened
D) Waiting to see whether the breach becomes public
E) Asking the affected people to keep the breach secret
= A, B
? Many data protection laws require notification of the authority within a short deadline (often 72 hours) and of the affected people when there is a high risk to them.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
Before maintenance work on a chemical line, what must be in place?
A) A permit to work, with isolation and gas testing confirmed
B) A verbal agreement between the operator and the fitter at the start of the shift
C) A note in the shift log that the work will take place
D) An email from the project manager approving the work
= A
? A permit-to-work system controls hazardous work by confirming isolations, testing and precautions before work starts.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
Lecturers upload chapters of copyrighted textbooks to the new learning platform without permission. What should the project do?
A) Agree a copyright and licence check with the library before publishing
B) Allow the uploads, since educational use is always exempt from copyright law
C) Block all uploads to the platform until the end of the academic year
D) Ask the students to report any copyrighted material that they find
= A
? Copyright compliance is built into the process, with the right experts, so that lecturers can share material lawfully.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
Aviation rules limit crew duty and flight times. How should the new crew scheduling system address these rules?
A) Implement them as requirements and test them against the regulations before go-live
B) Let the schedulers check the rules manually, since the system only proposes the rosters
C) Implement the rules after go-live, once the system has proved that it works in practice
D) Ask the vendor to confirm that its standard product already meets the regulations
= A
? Regulatory rules become requirements, verified through testing against the regulations themselves before the system is used.

@@ BE2 | Agile
Team members sign up for a free online tool to share files with a vendor, outside the approved IT channels. What should the scrum master do?
A) Switch to an approved tool, or request approval through the proper process
B) Allow it, since the tool is free and makes collaboration with the vendor easier
C) Allow it for non-confidential files only, without telling the IT department
D) Ask the vendor to choose a file-sharing tool that suits it best
= A
? Unapproved tools can breach security and data rules. Teams use approved tools or obtain approval, even when it is less convenient.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
In an industry consortium, a competitor proposes that members share their future pricing plans. What should the project manager do?
A) Decline and consult legal, since sharing such information may break competition law
B) Share the plans, since the consortium agreement covers all information exchanges
C) Share only approximate prices, so that no exact figures are disclosed to competitors
D) Ask the other members whether they are willing to share their plans first
= A
? Sharing competitively sensitive information, such as future prices, with competitors can breach competition (antitrust) law.

@@ BE3 | Agile
A security incident reveals a type of vulnerability that affects many features. How should the work be handled?
A) The product owner reprioritises the backlog, using the agreed expedite route
B) The team finishes the current sprint as planned and adds the fixes at the end of the release
C) The security team fixes the code itself without telling the product owner or the developers
D) The team stops all work until every feature has been rewritten from the beginning
= A
? Urgent, high-value work is handled through the backlog and the team's agreed policies for expedited work, so that priorities stay transparent.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
After go-live, a scheduler wants to adjust a crew rule parameter in the live system. What should happen?
A) The change goes through the operational change process, with testing and approval
B) The scheduler makes the change directly, since schedulers use the system daily
C) The vendor makes the change during its next scheduled maintenance window next month
D) The change is made and reported at the next monthly operations meeting
= A
? Changes to configuration that affects compliance are controlled through change management, with testing and approval before implementation.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
Before a major change to the student system, what should be prepared in case the change fails?
A) A tested backout plan
B) A press release
C) A new project charter
D) A lessons learned report
= A
? A tested backout (rollback) plan allows the team to restore the previous state quickly if the change causes problems.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
What should be the main basis for the change control board's decision on a change request?
A) Its impact on the project's objectives and value, compared with its costs and risks
B) The seniority of the person who submitted the change request to the board
C) The number of change requests that the board has already approved this year
D) Whether the project team would enjoy working on the requested change
= A
? Change decisions weigh the change's contribution to objectives and value against its impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk.

@@ BE3 | Agile
In the middle of a sprint, the team learns of a regulatory change that must be implemented next month. What should happen?
A) The product owner adds it to the product backlog and orders it so that it is done in time
B) The team stops the current sprint immediately and implements the change right away
C) The team ignores the change until the regulator asks whether it has been made, to avoid rework
D) The scrum master assigns the change to the most experienced developer at once
= A
? New requirements enter the product backlog, where the product owner orders them by value and urgency, usually for an upcoming sprint.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Testers cannot use production data because of privacy rules, and testing is blocked. What should the team do?
A) Create anonymised or synthetic test data, agreed with the data protection officer
B) Copy the production data into the test environment quietly, just for this release
C) Skip the tests that need realistic data and test those features in production after the release
D) Wait until the privacy rules change before continuing with the blocked testing
= A
? The impediment is removed in a compliant way: synthetic or anonymised data enables realistic testing without breaching privacy rules.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
Critical instruments are held at customs because some shipping documents are missing. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with the supplier and customs agent to supply the documents, and assess the impact
B) Order new instruments from another supplier, since the shipment may take months to release
C) Wait for customs to contact the project, since customs procedures are outside its control
D) Ask the customs officers to release the instruments without the missing documents
= A
? Issues are resolved by working with the parties involved to remove the cause, while assessing and managing the impact on the project.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
Several key academic approvers are on sabbatical, and their sign-offs are delayed. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree delegates with authority through the faculty's governance
B) Proceed without the sign-offs and inform the approvers on their return
C) Wait until the approvers return from their sabbaticals
D) Ask the approvers' students to sign on their behalf
= A
? Decision bottlenecks are resolved by agreeing properly authorised delegates, not by bypassing approvals.

@@ BE4 | Predictive | multi
Which two factors are typically used to prioritise issues in the issue log? (Choose two.)
A) The impact on the project's objectives
B) The urgency of resolving the issue
C) The seniority of the person who raised it
D) The date on which the issue was raised
E) The alphabetical order of issue titles
= A, B
? Issues are prioritised by their impact and urgency, so that the most important problems are resolved first.

@@ BE4 | Agile
When should a scrum master escalate an impediment beyond the team?
A) When the team cannot remove it and it threatens the sprint goal or delivery
B) Immediately, every time a team member mentions any kind of problem
C) Never, since self-managing teams must solve all of their own problems without outside help
D) Only at the end of the release, when all of the impediments are known
= A
? The team tries to remove impediments itself first. Those beyond its control that threaten its goals are escalated promptly.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
An issue that was marked as resolved has reappeared twice. What should the project manager do?
A) Reopen it, analyse the root cause and verify the fix before closing it again
B) Close it again, since it was resolved successfully on both previous occasions
C) Create a new issue each time it reappears, so that the log stays accurate
D) Remove it from the log, since recurring issues distort the project's statistics
= A
? A recurring issue shows that the earlier fixes treated symptoms. Root cause analysis and verification prevent further recurrence.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
Three risks have the following probabilities and impacts: 10% and $200,000; 30% and $50,000; 5% and $1,000,000. What contingency reserve do their expected monetary values suggest? (Answer in dollars.)
= $85,000
? EMV = 0.10 × 200,000 + 0.30 × 50,000 + 0.05 × 1,000,000 = 20,000 + 15,000 + 50,000 = $85,000.

@@ BE5 | Agile
A critical feature depends on an open-source library maintained by a single volunteer. What should the team do?
A) Assess the risk and plan responses, such as preparing an alternative
B) Ignore the risk, since open-source libraries are always maintained by the community
C) Remove the feature from the product immediately to avoid any dependency
D) Copy the library's code into the product and stop tracking its updates
= A
? Dependencies on fragile external components are risks. Responses can include monitoring, contributing to the library or preparing alternatives.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
Which response best addresses the risk of losing student records?
A) Regular backups with tested recovery procedures
B) An insurance policy that covers lost student records
C) A rule that staff must be careful with the records
D) Storing all records on one very reliable server
= A
? Backups reduce the impact of data loss, but only tested recovery procedures prove that data can actually be restored.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Which technique shows a hazardous event with its causes and preventive barriers on one side, and its consequences and mitigating barriers on the other?
A) A bow-tie analysis
B) A Pareto chart
C) A burnup chart
D) A fault tree analysis
= A
? A bow-tie diagram places the hazardous event at the centre, with threats and preventive barriers on the left and consequences and mitigating barriers on the right.

@@ BE5 | Agile
With limited time, how should the team decide where to focus its testing?
A) On the areas with the highest risk, based on the likelihood and impact of failure
B) On the areas that are easiest to test, so that more of the tests can be completed in time
C) On the areas that the developers changed least during the last sprint
D) Equally on every area, so that no part of the product is treated differently
= A
? Risk-based testing directs limited effort to where failures are most likely and most harmful.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Which chart ranks the risks or variables that have the greatest effect on a project's outcome, from largest to smallest?
A) A tornado diagram
B) A histogram
C) A Gantt chart
D) A cause-and-effect diagram
= A
? A tornado diagram displays the results of sensitivity analysis, with the most influential variables at the top.

@@ BE6 | Agile
The team deliberately causes failures in a controlled way to find weaknesses in its systems before real incidents do. What is this practice called?
A) Chaos engineering
B) Gold plating
C) Fast tracking
D) Regression testing
= A
? Chaos engineering, sometimes run as "game days", tests resilience through controlled experiments and leads to improvements.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
A serious accident has occurred at another company's similar plant. How should the project use this?
A) Review the published findings and apply the relevant lessons to the design and procedures
B) Ignore it, since the accident happened at another company with different staff
C) Wait for the regulator to issue new rules before considering any of the lessons from the accident
D) Use it only in the project's public communications to show that the plant is safer
= A
? Learning from external incidents is a powerful improvement practice, especially in high-hazard industries.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
Project managers say that the PMO's templates are slow and difficult to use. What should the PMO do?
A) Gather feedback, simplify the templates and measure the effect
B) Make the templates mandatory and audit their use more often in every project
C) Remove all templates and let each project work as it wishes
D) Ask the project managers to attend training on the templates
= A
? Continuous improvement applies to the organisation's own processes: feedback, small changes and measurement improve them.

@@ BE6 | Agile
A team describes where it wants its process to be in three months and works towards it in small experiments, step by step. What is this approach called?
A) The improvement kata
B) The critical path method
C) Rolling wave planning
D) Earned value management
= A
? The improvement kata sets a target condition and approaches it through repeated small experiments and reflection.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Small groups of front-line workers meet regularly to identify and solve problems in their own work. What is this practice called?
A) Quality circles
B) Steering committees
C) Change control boards
D) Phase gate reviews
= A
? Quality circles involve the people who do the work in continuous improvement, using their knowledge of the process.

@@ BE7 | Agile
Employees keep clicking on phishing emails. Which approach is most likely to change their behaviour over time?
A) Regular, realistic simulations with immediate, supportive training
B) A single long training session once a year for all staff
C) Publicly naming the employees who click on phishing emails
D) Block all external emails from reaching employees' inboxes until behaviour improves
= A
? Behaviour changes through frequent practice and immediate, constructive feedback, not through shame or one-off training.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
After go-live, staff still follow the old procedures because the official procedures were never updated. What should the project do?
A) Update the procedures, roles and training as part of the change
B) Tell the staff that they must follow the new system, whatever the procedures say
C) Leave the procedures as they are, since the staff will adapt over time
D) Ask each department to write its own procedures without any guidance
= A
? Organisational change includes aligning formal procedures, roles and training with the new way of working.

@@ BE7 | Predictive | match
Match each change model with its focus.
1) ADKAR
2) Kotter's eight steps
3) Lewin's three stages
4) Bridges' transition model
A) The stages an individual goes through to change
B) The steps leaders take to drive change in an organisation
C) Unfreezing, changing and refreezing behaviour
D) Endings, the neutral zone and new beginnings
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Different change models highlight different aspects of change: individual stages, leadership steps, organisational stages or people's psychological transitions.

@@ BE7 | Agile
The organisation is moving from temporary project teams to long-lived product teams. Which change is most important to support this?
A) Funding persistent teams and defining product ownership roles
B) Renaming the project managers as product managers
C) Keeping the annual project funding process as it is
D) Asking each team to choose a new name and logo
= A
? Moving to a product operating model requires changes to funding, roles and governance, not just new titles.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Training on the new procedures is scheduled only on weekday mornings, but the operators work rotating shifts. What should the project do?
A) Schedule training for every shift, including nights and weekends
B) Ask the night-shift operators to come in on their days off
C) Train only the day-shift operators and let them teach the others
D) Send the night-shift operators the slides to read at home
= A
? Change activities must reach everyone affected, which means fitting training to how people actually work.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
New regulations will restrict how the plant can transport chlorine to its customers. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact on operations and the project with logistics and compliance
B) Continue as planned, since the project upgrades the plant and not the transport arrangements
C) Ask the customers to collect the chlorine themselves so that the rules do not apply
D) Wait until the new transport rules are in force before assessing what they mean
= A
